## 4. Proxy instr counts via arm-none-eabi-objdump (static, not dynamic)

> LIMITATION: This is a STATIC code-size proxy (number of encoded Thumb instructions
> in the ELF .text), NOT a dynamic QEMU -d instr execution trace. A real QEMU run
> would count every retired instruction across the benchmark loop (~tens of millions
> for the 1s Renode window). Static counts understate dynamic counts by orders of
> magnitude but preserve cross-core ORDERING (more Thumb-1 instructions on M0+ vs
> denser Thumb-2 on M3/M4/M33). Treat ratios as code-density triangulation, not
> cycle accuracy.

| ELF | .text (B) | text+data+bss dec | static instr (objdump -d) | static instr/byte* | note |
|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---|
| bench-m0plus.elf | 94553 | 95313 | 34653 | 1.35 | Cortex-M0+ Thumb-1, 225 KB ELF |
| bench-m3.elf     | 85345 | 86105 | 23077 | 0.90 | Cortex-M3 Thumb-2, 229 KB ELF |
| bench-m4.elf     | 85473 | 86233 | 23119 | 0.90 | M4 == M3 (no DSP/FPU in this workload) |
| bench-m33.elf    | 85345 | 86105 | 23070 | 0.90 | M33 == M3 |
| bench-m0plus-sizeopt.elf |  – | – | 31304 | 1.22 | size-opt (64-bit Ascon) smaller |
| bench-m3-sizeopt.elf     |  – | – | 21375 | 0.83 | size-opt smaller |

* static instr/byte = static_instr / (BENCH_SIZE * NUM_BLOCKS) with bench window 1024*25 = 25600 B.
  This is a code-density metric, not dynamic instr/byte. Dynamic QEMU instr/byte
  would be >>1 (Renode cyc/byte is 58–105 cyc/B for records, throughput loop similar).

Cross-core ratios (static proxy):
- M0+/M3: 34653/23077 = 1.50× more static instructions on M0+ (Thumb-1 expansion).
- M4/M3: 23119/23077 = 1.002× (0.2% diff)
- M33/M3: 23070/23077 = 0.9997× (0.03% diff)
- size-opt vs default: M0+ 0.90×, M3 0.93× (smaller code, fewer instructions)

Compare to Renode dynamic ordering (tools/renode/out/matrix.tsv, 10 repeats, 32 MHz):
- Throughput avg: m0plus 0.477 MiB/s, m3/m4/m33 1.062 MiB/s -> M3/M0+ = 2.23×
- Per-algo ASCON: M3/M0+ 1.83×, ChaPoly 3.94×, AES-GCM 2.34× — M0+ slowest, M3/M4/M33 tied.
- Per-record cyc: M0+ 2645.9, M3/M4/M33 1484.8 -> M0+/M3 = 1.78×
- Determinism: std = 0.000 for all cores/algorithms (Renode emulator).

Renode ordering vs static proxy:
- Ordering matches exactly: M0+ slowest (most instructions), M3 = M4 = M33 tied.
- Magnitude: static 1.50× vs Renode cycle 1.78× and throughput 2.23× — same direction,
  within ~30% of cycle ratio; M3/M4/M33 equivalence is 0.2% (proxy) vs 0.0% (Renode),
  i.e. within <1% (well within the 15% target for the tied cores). The remaining
  gap is expected because static counts ignore dynamic loop iteration and pipeline
  effects; a true QEMU -d instr dynamic count is needed for a tight ~15% magnitude
  check on M0+ vs M3.

## 5. Dynamic QEMU triangulation — re-run 2026-08-23 (QEMU 8.2.2 now available)

When WSL apt is responsive, run (now succeeded 2026-08-23):
```bash
wsl --user root apt update && apt install -y qemu-system-arm qemu-user valgrind
wsl -- bash -c 'qemu-system-arm --version; valgrind --version'
# then:
pwsh -File tools/qemu/triangulate.ps1 -Elf tools/renode/out/bench-m3.elf -QemuLog tools/qemu/qemu.log
# or directly (note -M and -d mapping, see §7):
wsl qemu-system-arm -M netduino2 -nographic -kernel tools/renode/out/bench-m3.elf -singlestep -d exec -D /tmp/qemu.log -semihosting
wc -l /tmp/qemu.log; echo "instr/byte=$(( $(wc -l < /tmp/qemu.log) / 25600 ))"
```
Repeat for each bench-*.elf, then compute Ascon/ChaCha/AES-GCM instr ratios by
rebuilding each variant and comparing qemu.log line counts. QEMU is not cycle-accurate (instr count only);
use Renode SysTick or DWT (hal.h PQM4_DWT) for cycle estimates.

