## 7. Dynamic QEMU triangulation — results 2026-08-23 (success, caveats)

### 7.1 WSL verification (Task 1 post-install)
```bash
wsl -- bash -c 'which qemu-system-arm; qemu-system-arm --version; which qemu-arm; qemu-arm --version; which valgrind; valgrind --version'
# /usr/bin/qemu-system-arm, QEMU 8.2.2 (Debian 1:8.2.2+ds-0ubuntu1.18)
# /usr/bin/qemu-arm, qemu-arm version 8.2.2
# /usr/bin/valgrind, valgrind-3.22.0
wsl --user root -- bash -c 'dpkg -l | grep -E "qemu|valgrind"'
# ii qemu-system-arm 1:8.2.2+ds-0ubuntu1.18, ii qemu-system-common, ii qemu-system-data, ii qemu-user, ii valgrind 1:3.22.0-0ubuntu3
```

### 7.2 QEMU system-mode caveats (discovered)
- `qemu-system-arm -d instr` is NOT valid for system-mode (only user-mode `qemu-arm -d instr`). System-mode valid `-d` includes `exec,in_asm,cpu,mmu,guest_errors,...` (see `qemu-system-arm -d help`). The triangulate.ps1 `-d instr` therefore produces no log and prints the help list; it is a documentation bug for system-mode.
- With `-M mps2-an385` (and an386/an505/500/musca) the bench ELFs fault immediately: `Lockup: can't escalate 3 to HardFault, R15=00000000` after 10 lines of exec trace. Reason: FLASH is at 0x08000000 in bench.ld, but mps2-an385 maps flash at 0x00000000/0x01000000, so vector fetch fails. The working boards for this layout are `netduino2`, `netduinoplus2`, `stm32vldiscovery` (all map 0x08000000 correctly; entry 0x080003f4 shows as `[00800400/00000000080003f4/...] reset_handler`).
- `netduino2` is fixed to `cortex-m3` (board restriction `This board can only be used with CPU cortex-m3-arm-cpu`), so running bench-m0plus on it still counts as M3 — it does not differentiate Thumb-1 vs Thumb-2 code density. For true M0 vs M3 differentiation, keep static proxy (§4). No QEMU board exposes `cortex-m0` with 0x08000000 flash in this QEMU build; `microbit` (M0) also faults (flash 0x00000000).
- Bench never exits: `reset_handler: bl main; b 5b` loops forever after `hdr[3]=1`. QEMU therefore never exits via semihosting; must use `timeout N qemu-system-arm ...` to stop. Full traces are 850M for 15 s (10M lines).
- To count instructions (not TBs), use `-singlestep` with `-d exec`: without it, `-d exec` logs one line per translation block (TB, 92–355 lines in 8 s). With `-singlestep`, each `Trace 0: ... [00800400/00000000080003f4/...]` is one retired instruction.

### 7.3 Dynamic counts (singlestep exec, counts are per-retired-instruction lines)
```bash
# All on netduino2 (M3), singlestep exec, timeout 8 s wall (bench throughput + record + idle spin)
# Full logs at /tmp/qemu-*.log (not committed; summary in tools/qemu/qemu.log)
for core in m0plus m3 m4 m33; do
  timeout 8 qemu-system-arm -M netduino2 -nographic -kernel tools/renode/out/bench-$core.elf -singlestep -d exec -D /tmp/qemu-$core.log -semihosting
  wc -l /tmp/qemu-$core.log
done
```
| ELF | wall | log | lines (singlestep exec) | TB (no singlestep) | note |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| bench-m0plus.elf | 8 s | /tmp/qemu-m0plus.log | 8217759 | 92 | M0+ binary on M3 CPU (density not differentiated) |
| bench-m3.elf | 8 s | /tmp/qemu-m3.log | 8039624 | 355 | M3, netduino2 |
| bench-m3.elf (stm32vldiscovery) | 8 s | /tmp/qemu-stm32.log | 5198821 | – | same ELF, different board timing (fewer instr/s) |
| bench-m4.elf | 8 s | /tmp/qemu-m4.log | 7555761 | 93 | M4 binary on M3 CPU |
| bench-m33.elf | 8 s | /tmp/qemu-m33.log | 7985564 | 355 | M33 binary on M3 CPU |
| bench-m3.elf | 15 s | /tmp/qemu-m3-singlestep.log | 10354244 | – | 850M file, head reset_handler, tail default_handler spin at 0x0800041e |

Without singlestep (TB): 92,355,93,355 lines — vs singlestep 7.5–8.2M — TB << instr, as expected. Static proxy 23k–34k is code size, dynamic 8M is retired instructions across the window (bench loop >>1 iteration), so dynamic >> static (orders of magnitude) as predicted in §4.

Ratios (8 s window, netduino2, singlestep):
- M0+/M3: 8217759/8039624 = 1.022× (2.2% higher) — on same M3 CPU, not the 1.50× Thumb-1 expansion seen in static proxy; static proxy remains authoritative for M0+ vs M3 code density.
- M4/M3: 7555761/8039624 = 0.940× (6% lower) — artifact of running M4 binary on M3 CPU (no DSP), not a real core difference.
- M33/M3: 7985564/8039624 = 0.993× (0.7% diff) — tied, matching static 0.9997× and Renode 0.0%.
- M3 netduino2 vs stm32: 8039624/5198821 = 1.55× — board-dependent TB chaining/timing, not core; shows QEMU is triangulation only, not cycle-accurate.

Comparison to static and Renode:
- Static proxy correctly shows M0+ > M3 (1.50×) and M3=M4=M33 tied (0.2% diff), matching Renode ordering (M0+ slowest 0.477 MiB/s vs M3 1.062 MiB/s, 2.23×). QEMU dynamic on fixed M3 CPU cannot replicate M0+ Thumb-1 overhead, so it shows ~1.02× — this is a QEMU board limitation, not a contradiction.
- For M3/M4/M33 equivalence, QEMU dynamic 0.94–0.99× matches static 0.999× and Renode 1.00× within <15% (well within target). The remaining gap is idle spin after hdr[3]=1 (b 5b) included in wall window; a proper exit via semihosting `bkpt 0xAB` or rebuilding with `__specs=rdimon` would give bench-only counts. Future work: rebuild bench with semihosting exit, then `timeout` not needed.

### 7.4 Triangulation conclusion
- QEMU is now installed and produces dynamic exec traces (see tools/qemu/qemu.log for summary, /tmp/qemu-*.log for raw). The traces confirm dynamic >> static (8M vs 23k) and provide TB vs instr distinction.
- For cross-core ordering, static proxy (§4) + Renode matrix remain authoritative; QEMU dynamic on netduino2 confirms M3/M4/M33 tied within <15% but cannot differentiate M0+ on M3 board. This is documented as a QEMU board mapping ceiling (ponytail: fixed M3 board, per-core boards if throughput differentiation needed).
- QEMU is triangulation only — not cycle-accurate; use Renode DWT (hal.h PQM4_DWT) for cycles. Ascon instr/byte dynamic with singlestep 8M/25600 ≈ 312 instr/byte in this window (includes idle), vs static 0.90 instr/byte (code density) — the large gap is expected.

